"passage": "'Though the sun doth set on paths of gold, I stop to see if my passing kindness may bestow some fortune to those, whom chance hath lost in shadows laid. Walks and roads part our ways, yet virtues teach us ever, to observe. In this vale of late, have I laid eyes upon a purse, of silver's hue, which gold did ne'er outshine. Cursed are those that shun to seek an owner, for in a heart-of-honesty lies the voice of alms and grace. To leave it there in sin, let it lodge in a wallet of its own, a thief's, who'll rust new gleam. Onward, towards it shines the light of virtue, to my own name and me, a finder, not a thief, may I be found. Patience, trust, and grace: such have I gain'd since making an honest pledge.'", "terminated": true, "judge": {"id": "validation-0-0", "scores": {"coherence": 3.9985430240631104, "fluency": 4.012129783630371, "prompt_fulfillment": 4.562173843383789}, "rating_token_mass": {"coherence": 1.0, "fluency": 1.0, "prompt_fulfillment": 1.0}, "rating_probabilities": {"coherence": [5.59412915990265e-09, 5.035674348619068e-07, 0.0015011134091764688, 0.9984530210494995, 4.5329699787544087e-05], "fluency": [1.6112768719267478e-07, 3.667249075078871e-06, 0.0018996821017935872, 0.9840596914291382, 0.014036857523024082], "prompt_fulfillment": [3.319816921365515e-10, 7.55586881950876e-09, 1.8488601654098602e-06, 0.43782269954681396, 0.562175452709198]}, "quality": 0.797737220923106}}
